// The oceanwaves package (a working name; Jon names packages): an FFT ocean, calm to storm. A program
// reaches it with 'import oceanwaves' and gets a sea it can step, query and draw:
//
//   imm sea = SeaState.beaufort(7, 0.);                    // force 7, the waves travelling toward +x
//   mut ocean = Ocean.make(OceanSpec.forSea(sea, 21u32));  // three FFT cascades, a seed
//   ocean.update(t);                                       // every frame: the surface at time t
//   ocean.heightAt(x, z);  ocean.normalAt(x, z);  ocean.foamAt(x, z);
//   ocean.setSea(SeaState.beaufort(9, 0.));                // the wind rises: the same waves, grown
//   mut sr = OceanRenderer.create(&mut renderer, &mut device, &ocean, OceanLook.temperate(&sky));
//   sr.upload(&mut renderer, &mut device, &ocean);  sr.addToList(&mut list, cameraX, cameraZ);
//
// WHAT IT DOES
//
//   SPECTRUM (spectrum.cone). JONSWAP (Hasselmann et al. 1973), the spectrum of a sea still growing under
//   the wind over a limited fetch, falling back to Pierson-Moskowitz (1964) for a fully developed one;
//   spread in direction by the Hasselmann/Mitsuyasu cos^2s form in Horvath's (2015) parameterisation; the
//   dispersion relation w^2 = g k tanh(k d). 'SeaState.beaufort(n, direction)' gives forces 0 to 12;
//   'significantHeight' integrates the spectrum (a fully developed 20 m/s sea comes out within 15
//   percent of Pierson-Moskowitz's 0.0246 U^2).
//
//   SURFACE (cascade.cone, fft.cone). Tessendorf's method ("Simulating Ocean Water", 2001): a sum of waves
//   with Gaussian random amplitudes scaled by the spectrum, evolved by the dispersion relation and summed
//   by inverse FFT. Three CASCADES (tiles of different sizes, each carrying its own band of wavelengths)
//   resolve a swell hundreds of metres long and ripples under a metre together, the technique of the
//   ocean in Crysis and in Assassin's Creed IV. Four inverse FFTs a cascade give eight fields: height,
//   horizontal displacement (the CHOPPINESS that sharpens crests), slopes, and the derivatives of the
//   displacement, whose Jacobian says where the surface folds.
//
//   FOAM and SPRAY (ocean.cone). Foam is made where the Jacobian of the whole surface's displacement is
//   low (Tessendorf; Jensen and Golias, GPU Gems ch. 1), persists and fades; 'sprayAt' lists the points
//   where waves are breaking hardest, with the crest's direction, for an emitter to turn into particles:
//   vfx's Event.at(id, time, Mat4.fromTranslation(spot.position)) with a spray Emitter.
//
//   HEIGHT QUERY (ocean.cone). 'heightAt(x, z)' inverts the displacement by fixed-point iteration and
//   reads the height there: what a floating body floats on is exactly the surface that is drawn.
//
//   RENDERING (render.cone, ocean.slang). One custom pipeline of render: a fine mesh of 1.3 m cells round
//   the camera displaced in the vertex shader from the cascades' textures, a coarse 48 m ring beyond it,
//   shaded per fragment: Fresnel (Schlick, F0 0.02) between the reflected sky and sun and the body of the
//   water, a subsurface tint through crests, foam, aerial perspective. The fields cross to the GPU as
//   r32float textures once a frame ('upload').
//
// WHERE THE WORK RUNS. The FFTs, the foam and the height query run on the CPU, in Cone (5.4 ms a frame for
// three 128 x 128 cascades, measured), because the physics that floats bodies needs the heights on the CPU
// and the spectrum evolution is the cost; the shading runs on the GPU (0.2 ms on the RTX, 3 to 5 ms on the
// Intel UHD 770 at 1280 x 720). A GPU FFT is the next step (see the Design note).
//
// FILES. Only this root imports.
//   spectrum.cone   SeaState: JONSWAP, spreading, dispersion; angularFrequency, groupVelocity
//   fft.cone        Fft: radix-2, 1-D and 2-D inverse
//   cascade.cone    CascadeSpec, Cascade: the tile's waves and the eight fields
//   ocean.cone      OceanSpec, Ocean: cascades summed, queries, foam, spray
//   render.cone     OceanLook, OceanRenderer, the embedded shader (ocean.slang)
//
// CONVENTIONS: geomath's (right-handed, y up, metres, seconds, f32). A wave vector's direction is its
// direction of travel; 'windDirection' is an angle from +x toward +z.

mod oceanwaves;

import libc;
import collections use *;
import geomath use *;
import noise;
import time;
import mesh use Mesh;
import gpu use *;
import render use *;
